WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.560
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.160
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.840
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:11.760
In other words,

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.760
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.640
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.840
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.280
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.480
Today we have the second in our
two-part series on anti-aging creams.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.960
And we start with
a look at niacinamide,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.080
also known as nicotinamide,
a form of vitamin B3.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.680
I’ve talked about sunscreen
and topical retinoids.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.760
What other skin cream components have
been shown to help with skin aging?

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.160
While placebo-controlled trials
are the standard in most medical research,

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.640
they are still all too rare
for cosmetic products.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.600
This raises efficacy questions—

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.120
many are left with simply
buying “hope in a jar”—

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.520
as well as safety concerns.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.080
Cosmetics to this day contain
an array of toxic chemicals.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.400
Of the more than 12,000
synthetic chemicals used in cosmetics,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.560
fewer than 20 percent
have been recognized as safe.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.960
Of course, this doesn’t mean natural
ingredients are necessarily harmless.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.800
Poison ivy is as natural
as you can get,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.880
but you wouldn’t want
to rub in on your face.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.160
However, there are some relatively
safe natural options

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.640
with varying degrees of efficacy.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.280
In contrast to retinoic acid—

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.320
a form of vitamin A
that can be irritating—

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.880
topical niacinamide
(also known as nicotinamide)

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.960
is a form of vitamin B3
that is non-irritating

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.640
and found to be effective.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.800
It’s been described
as one of the best studied

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.720
cosmeceutical ingredients
for anti-aging,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.160
but it looks like there are only
two placebo-controlled human studies,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.640
which gives you an idea of the state
of cosmeceutical science.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:51.120
Skin photoaging is largely mediated
by UV-induced free radical formation.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.320
One of the consequences
of excess sun exposure

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.640
is the oxidation of sugars and proteins
in the skin into yellow-brown pigment

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.400
that gives aging skin
a yellowing, sallow appearance.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:05.600
Since niacinamide is a precursor
to two potent antioxidants,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.480
the hope is that this process
could be interrupted,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.200
and indeed the first published
study was entitled

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.320
“Topical niacinamide reduces yellowing,
wrinkling, red blotchiness,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.000
and hyperpigmented spots
in aging facial skin.”

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.400
It was a 12-week, double-blind,
placebo-controlled,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.280
randomized clinical split-face study
of middle-aged women.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.840
In a split-face study,
each woman is her own control,

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:30.320
rubbing the active formulation

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.160
(in this case 5% niacinamide
in moisturizer) on one side of her face

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.440
and the placebo (straight moisturizer)
on the other half,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.920
though neither she nor the researchers
know which side is which

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.360
until the code
is broken at the end.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.640
This controls for skin type
and administration technique

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.760
(different people apply
facial products differently),

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.680
but participants often use the same hand
for applying the creams to both sides;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.120
so, unless specified
that different gloves be worn,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.160
or hands washed in between,
there can be cross-contamination.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.680
Anyway, at the end of 12 weeks
there was a small, 5 percent reduction

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.560
in wrinkles and fine lines
and a slowing in the development

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.240
in blotchiness, spots, and sallowness.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.400
A subsequent publication noted
an improvement in skin elasticity as well.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.560
The magnitude of these
effects may only be

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.640
one-third to one-fifth
as good as retinoic acid,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.280
but there were no reports
of excess skin irritation.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.840
But use a four percent
concentration and application

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.080
was limited to crow’s feet wrinkles
around the eyes.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.560
Significant reductions in wrinkles
by both subjective and objective measures

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.080
were noted by the end
of the eight-week study.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.920
Sixty-four percent
of the niacinamide-side eye wrinkles

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.960
underwent moderate
or marked improvement

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.320
compared to zero percent
on the placebo-side eyes.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:44.960
Finally today,

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.400
we look at the pros and cons
of alpha hydroxy acid lotions,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.400
chemical peels,
and topical antioxidants

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.240
in reversing the
signs of aging.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.800
There is a reason why
there is a long historical use

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.280
of fruit purees as facial masks,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.520
and perhaps why Cleopatra
was said to bathe in sour milk.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.600
Alpha hydroxy acids,
also known as fruit acids,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.440
are natural acids found in foods.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.920
They include citric acid (from citrus),
glycolic acid (from sugar cane),

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.840
lactic acid (from fermented fruits),
malic acid(from a variety of fruits),

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.400
and tartaric acid (from grapes).

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.760
High strength concentrations
are used for chemical peels.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.960
Concentrations over 40%
can only be used by medical doctors.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.480
Professionals in salons can give more
mild peels with 10 to 40% acid solutions,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.200
but concentrations under 10% are sold
over the counter as exfoliants.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.520
Alpha hydroxy acids are thought to work
by weakening cell-to-cell bonds

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:43.440
to hasten the shedding of dead cells
off the skin surface.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.920
Three placebo-controlled studies
of over-the-counter strengths

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:46.800
have been published.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.640
The first compared an 8% glycolic acid
lotion to an 8% lactic acid lotion

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.520
to a placebo lotion applied daily
on face and forearms for 22 weeks.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.480
Both the acids worked similarly,
producing visible improvements

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.720
in facial photodamage in more than
70% of the acid groups

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.320
versus only 40% of the placebo group.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:05.760
All the participants were advised

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.120
to wear protective clothing
and regularly use sunscreen,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.360
which may help explain the benefits
even in the placebo group.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.640
Overall forearm photodamage and sallowness

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.840
also improved significantly
in the acid versus placebo groups.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.240
About 1 in 3 participants
experienced transient redness,

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.880
but only 1 (out of 74) left the study
due to facial irritation.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.680
The second trial found a 5% glycolic acid
lotion for three months

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.320
beat out placebo on lessening skin
roughness and mottled discoloration

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:38.240
on the face and neck, but failed
to significantly reduce wrinkle scores.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.400
The irony of alpha hydroxy acids is that
while they can help with past photodamage,

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.360
they can make future damage worse
by increasing skin photosensitivity.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.200
So, taking precautions is recommended.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.800
The FDA recommends that all
alpha hydroxy products

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.480
be labeled with a prominent warning:

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.720
Sunburn Alert: This product may increase
your skin’s sensitivity to the sun

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:01.560
and particularly
the possibility of sunburn.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.640
Use a sunscreen,
wear protective clothing,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:08.000
and limit sun exposure while using
this product and for a week afterwards.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.640
What about ascorbic acid,
also known as vitamin C?

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.880
If niacinamide works
by turning into antioxidants,

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.320
why not just apply
antioxidants directly?

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.600
Topical application can lead
to vitamin E levels in the skin

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.000
10 times what is achieved by oral dosing
and vitamin C up to 40 times higher

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.520
(at least in mouse
and pig skin, respectively).

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.240
According to a review
on topical anti-aging skin care

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.360
by a prominent
Beverly Hills plastic surgeon,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.720
“At a minimum, patients should be
encouraged to use daily sunscreen,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.320
a topical retinoid every night,
and a topical antioxidant daily.”

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.440
But there’s only one antioxidant
that’s been clearly shown to work.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.600
Despite its ubiquity
in skin care products,

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.040
there is no evidence
to support any role

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:54.360
for topical vitamin E in skin aging,

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.240
whether for wrinkles,
discoloration, or texture.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.120
The one study on topical CoQ10

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.040
also failed to work
significantly better than placebo,

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:06.840
but there is one type of vitamin C
that has been shown to help.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.760
Skin biopsy studies show
that the topical application

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.760
of a 5% solution of L-ascorbic acid
(also known as just ascorbic acid,

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.960
the type of vitamin C found in food)

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:19.480
significantly increases the expression
of collagen in human skin

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.760
compared to placebo,

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.040
suggesting “functional activity
of the skin cells

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.440
is not maximal in postmenopausal women
and can be increased.”

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.840
A split-face study
involving the application

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.360
of three drops of a 10% L-ascorbic
solution for three months

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.080
found significant improvements
over the placebo side of the face

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.920
in fine and coarse wrinkles,
sallowness, and skin tone or firmness.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.800
Not knowing which side was which,
16 out of 19—84% of patients—

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:50.760
correctly identified the vitamin C side
as the one showing improvement.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.440
Unfortunately, L-ascorbic acid
is unstable in creams,

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:58.360
turning an unsightly brown when
it oxidizes, limiting its shelf life;

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.760
so instead, the skin care industry
uses more stable vitamin C esters

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.640
or derivatives such as ascorbyl palmitate,
magnesium ascorbyl phosphate,

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.840
ascorbic acid sulfate,
ascorbyl stearate, etc.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:13.000
Unfortunately, there is no evidence

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:14.920
that these compounds
have comparable effects,

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.640
likely because they are poorly absorbed

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.800
and only minimally
convert to the active form.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.600
The good news is that
you can make your own.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.940
Although vitamin C concentrations
as low as 3% or 5%

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.920
and have been shown
to have anti-wrinkle effects

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.040
in split face or split neck
and arm studies, 10% is recommended.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:42.280
The 10% solution used in this study retails
for a ridiculous $127 per ounce.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.920
You can make a DIY solution more than
two thousand times cheaper

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.720
simply by buying L-ascorbic acid in bulk
and mixing 3 grams into 30 grams of water

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.400
at a cost of about a nickel per ounce.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:55.680
You can mix it in an eyedropper

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.080
and drip four to five drops
on the palm of your hand

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.560
and use your fingertips to apply over
your face, neck, and upper chest daily.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.920
Just be careful to not
get any in your eyes.

